#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186356697 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a75b47ec-8d5a-545e-b915-daca92320322" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Non-Roots of a Polynomial Using the Rational Roots Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6780c823-b306-56d1-8eff-1a05bbae2d2b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356699} {:answer/id #uuid "bbcdf7c6-1596-591f-99dc-3c3d302885e2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356700} {:answer/id #uuid "88a0b791-070a-5678-9cc3-7cbca1836c02" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356701} {:answer/id #uuid "9518a177-d2d4-511e-8aaa-c5995d850ce7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356702} {:answer/id #uuid "4fb5649c-8f2d-5678-b6a1-730ea192e41a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356703}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6780c823-b306-56d1-8eff-1a05bbae2d2b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356699} :answer-field/id #uuid "8077c67b-86a5-5d48-8b0c-7df2ce37e9c0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356698}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d8ff66df-4977-5b8b-890c-17e01de26de7" :question/math-academy-id "q-112873" :question/problem "According to the rational roots theorem, which of the following **cannot** be a root of the polynomial $f(x)=8{x}^{3}-4x+5?$" :question/worked-solution "Given a polynomial with integer coefficients, the rational roots theorem states that any rational roots of the polynomial must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of the constant term}}{\\text{factor of the leading coefficient}}.\n$$\n\nFor the given polynomial $f(x),$ the constant term is $5$ and the leading coefficient is $8.$ So, any rational roots of $f(x)$ must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of }5}{\\text{factor of }8}.\n$$\n\nOf the given answer choices, only $\\frac{5}{6}$ does not take the above form. The denominator $(6)$ is not a factor of $8.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186356704 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "001262b8-82b7-5421-80ac-8608b20d4be1" :knowledge-point/title "Factoring a Cubic Polynomial Using the Rational Roots Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "95cc84a3-7d3d-58d3-9e86-4450e6289ea8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356706} {:answer/id #uuid "966d87a8-987b-57c0-a31e-38d67094453e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356707}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "966d87a8-987b-57c0-a31e-38d67094453e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356707} :answer-field/id #uuid "05b7a21e-a3c4-5d87-83c6-215690847613" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356705} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ca7fa490-f5fc-590b-ba89-6f0c2f7ed8c8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356709}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ca7fa490-f5fc-590b-ba89-6f0c2f7ed8c8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356709} :answer-field/id #uuid "1eab6f8c-fb5f-5d7a-80b3-7c3f501f2048" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356708}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "297bc86c-010a-5357-8616-cf63e473194e" :question/math-academy-id "q-275954" :question/problem "The polynomial $f(x)=2{x}^{3}-11{x}^{2}+20x-12$ has a root in the interval $(\\frac{5}{4},\\frac{5}{2}).$ In **ascending order**, the **distinct** real roots of $f(x)$ are\n\n$x=$\n {{field-1}}$,\\,x=$\n {{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "Given a polynomial with integer coefficients, the rational roots theorem states that any rational roots of the polynomial must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of the constant term}}{\\text{factor of the leading coefficient}}.\n$$\n\nFor the given polynomial $f(x),$ the constant term is $-12$ and the leading coefficient is $2.$ So, any rational roots of $f(x)$ must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of }-12}{\\text{factor of }2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the possible rational roots are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{1}{2},\\,\\pm 1,\\,\\pm \\frac{3}{2},\\,\\pm 2,\\,\\pm 3,\\,\\pm 4,\\,\\pm 6,\\,\\pm 12\n$$\n\nOf these roots, the only roots in the interval $(\\frac{5}{4},\\frac{5}{2})$ are $\\frac{3}{2}$ and $2.$\n\nNow, let's use synthetic division to test whether $(x-\\frac{3}{2})$ is a factor of $f(x).$ We get the following:\n\n|  | ${x}^{3}$ | ${x}^{2}$ | ${x}^{1}$ | ${x}^{0}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\frac{3}{2}$ | $2$ | $-11$ | $20$ | $-12$ |\n|  | $↓$ | $3$ | $-12$ | $12$ |\n|  | $2$ | $-8$ | $8$ | $0$ |\n\nSince the remainder is $0,$ we conclude that $(x-\\frac{3}{2})$ is indeed a factor of $f(x).$ Moreover, we have\n\n$$\nf(x)=(x-\\frac{3}{2})(2{x}^{2}-8x+8).\n$$\n\nSince the highest-degree factor is now a quadratic, we can continue factoring using the usual methods:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =(x-\\frac{3}{2})(2{x}^{2}-8x+8) \\\\  & =2(x-\\frac{3}{2})({x}^{2}-4x+4) \\\\  & =(2x-3){(x-2)}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the factors equal to $0,$ we find that, in ascending order, the roots of the polynomial are $x=\\frac{3}{2}$  and $x=2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186356710 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1b5f9ee9-a489-5048-8346-0116a19e9b4a" :knowledge-point/title "Counting the Number of Possible Rational Roots of a Polynomial"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dafd0e75-de9a-5941-9c87-35225447c01d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356712}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dafd0e75-de9a-5941-9c87-35225447c01d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356712} :answer-field/id #uuid "8f6b2ace-220d-5e68-a4e6-8b7ef7e7f437" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356711}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a8aff0b4-015d-5c83-900b-94f887fc4f2e" :question/math-academy-id "q-321645" :question/problem "According to the rational root theorem, the polynomial $f(x)=8{x}^{3}-3x+2$ has{{field-1}} *potential* rational roots." :question/worked-solution "Given a polynomial with integer coefficients, the rational roots theorem states that any rational roots of the polynomial must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of the constant term}}{\\text{factor of the leading coefficient}}.\n$$\n\nFor the given polynomial:\n\n- The constant term is $2,$ and its factors are $\\pm 1$ and $\\pm 2.$\n\n- The leading coefficient is $8,$ and its factors are $\\pm 1$, $\\pm 2$, $\\pm 4$, and $\\pm 8.$\n\nFirst, we list out all the possibilities for the numerator and denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\pm \\frac{1}{1},\\text{ }\\pm \\frac{1}{2},\\text{ }\\pm \\frac{1}{4},\\text{ }\\pm \\frac{1}{8}, \\\\ \\pm \\frac{2}{1},\\text{ }\\pm \\frac{2}{2},\\text{ }\\pm \\frac{2}{4},\\text{ }\\pm \\frac{2}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we reduce the fractions to their lowest terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\pm 1,\\text{ }\\pm \\frac{1}{2},\\text{ }\\pm \\frac{1}{4},\\text{ }\\pm \\frac{1}{8},\\text{ } \\\\ \\pm 2,\\text{ }\\pm 1,\\text{ }\\pm \\frac{1}{2},\\text{ }\\pm \\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that some potential roots are duplicates (shown above in red). Removing these duplicates and listing those remaining in order from least to greatest in absolute value, we have\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{1}{8},\\,\\pm \\frac{1}{4},\\,\\pm \\frac{1}{2},\\,\\pm 1,\\,\\pm 2.\n$$\n\nThis makes a total of $10$ potential roots. (Remember that each $\\pm $ sign accounts for $2$ roots, and $2\\cdot 5=10.$)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186356713 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a75b47ec-8d5a-545e-b915-daca92320322" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Non-Roots of a Polynomial Using the Rational Roots Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "163457f2-6548-5a9a-9b3f-6b7827f7da78" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356715} {:answer/id #uuid "c1f26617-4b91-5b60-b50a-e30200dfaf9c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356716} {:answer/id #uuid "07056a20-6b0b-5f6d-b143-01a25742c779" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356717} {:answer/id #uuid "e29ffd5e-fee2-5cf2-8700-7d068f661ef1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356718} {:answer/id #uuid "081f460e-e19c-5d73-9189-e2138e267107" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356719}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "07056a20-6b0b-5f6d-b143-01a25742c779" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356717} :answer-field/id #uuid "1d93d277-feb3-5525-9d4c-d8d49ee68342" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356714}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "032195d0-c32a-510e-9d2d-9923dbb6a6bb" :question/math-academy-id "q-6657" :question/problem "According to the rational roots theorem, which of the following **cannot** be a root of the polynomial $f(x)=2{x}^{3}+{x}^{2}-2x-1?$" :question/worked-solution "Given a polynomial with integer coefficients, the rational roots theorem states that any rational roots of the polynomial must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of the constant term}}{\\text{factor of the leading coefficient}}.\n$$\n\nFor the given polynomial $f(x),$ the constant term is $-1$ and the leading coefficient is $2.$ So, any rational roots of $f(x)$ must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of }-1}{\\text{factor of }2}.\n$$\n\nOf the given answer choices, only $2$ does not take the above form. The value $2$ can be interpreted as a fraction $\\frac{2}{1},$ and the numerator $(2)$ is not a factor of $-1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186356720 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "001262b8-82b7-5421-80ac-8608b20d4be1" :knowledge-point/title "Factoring a Cubic Polynomial Using the Rational Roots Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "faf4592b-d3e1-5234-bdee-38ed2d6320ec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356722} {:answer/id #uuid "54528da9-e463-5820-92f7-d6e4ae089aaa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356723} {:answer/id #uuid "09db3249-ee7b-5058-819d-4e53cbd0fb6f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356724} {:answer/id #uuid "2c4092e3-bf38-5bd0-89e0-61df52567a49" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356725} {:answer/id #uuid "de497e44-7ceb-5c84-aa9f-baa25d63cc83" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356726}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2c4092e3-bf38-5bd0-89e0-61df52567a49" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356725} :answer-field/id #uuid "b62557ab-2302-5fb0-aee8-07f656d07c3f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356721}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "fb97b373-7d53-58da-98de-e4426085b429" :question/math-academy-id "q-83864" :question/problem "Given that the polynomial $f(x)={x}^{3}+{x}^{2}-14x-24$ has one of its roots in the interval $(3,8),$ find the sum of the distinct real roots of $f(x).$" :question/worked-solution "Given a polynomial with integer coefficients, the rational roots theorem states that any rational roots of the polynomial must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of the constant term}}{\\text{factor of the leading coefficient}}.\n$$\n\nFor the given polynomial $f(x),$ the constant term is $-24$ and the leading coefficient is $1.$ So, any rational roots of $f(x)$ must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of }-24}{\\text{factor of }1}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the possible rational roots are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\pm 1,\\,\\pm 2,\\,\\pm 3,\\,\\pm 4,\\,\\pm 6,\\,\\pm 8,\\,\\pm 12,\\,\\pm 24\n$$\n\nOf these roots, the only roots in the interval $(3,8),$ are $4$ and $6.$\n\nNow, let's use synthetic division to test whether $(x-4)$ is a factor of $f(x).$ We get the following:\n\n|  | ${x}^{3}$ | ${x}^{2}$ | ${x}^{1}$ | ${x}^{0}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $4$ | $1$ | $1$ | $-14$ | $-24$ |\n|  | $↓$ | $4$ | $20$ | $24$ |\n|  | $1$ | $5$ | $6$ | $0$ |\n\nSince the remainder is $0,$ we conclude that $(x-4)$ is indeed a factor of $f(x).$ Moreover, we have\n\n$$\nf(x)=(x-4)({x}^{2}+5x+6).\n$$\n\nSince the highest-degree factor is now a quadratic, we can continue factoring using the usual methods:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =(x-4)({x}^{2}+5x+6) \\\\  & =(x-4)(x+2)(x+3)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the factors equal to $0,$ we find the roots $x=4,-2,-3.$ Summing these roots, we get\n\n$$\n4-2-3=-1.\n$$"}]}
